\section{Symbols in figures and PDF output}{

Figures use Unicode symbols (Greek letters such as rho and Delta, minus
and comparison signs, superscript exponents). The classic \code{\link[grDevices:pdf]{grDevices::pdf()}}
and \code{\link[grDevices:postscript]{grDevices::postscript()}} devices cannot draw characters outside
Latin-1; this is the device of \verb{R CMD check} and of \code{ggplot2::ggsave()} for
a \code{.pdf} file. The option \code{vernissage.unicode} controls the symbols:
\itemize{
\item \code{"auto"} (default): Unicode, except when the open device (or the default
device, when none is open) is a classic \code{pdf()} or \code{postscript()}
device; then ASCII equivalents are used (\code{rho}, \code{Delta}, \code{>=}, \code{x10^-4}).
\item \code{TRUE} / \code{FALSE}: always Unicode / always ASCII.
}

The choice is made when the figure is built. To keep the symbols in a PDF
file, save with a Cairo device, e.g.
\code{ggplot2::ggsave("figure.pdf", p, device = grDevices::cairo_pdf)}; to build
figures in an interactive session and save them later with \code{pdf()}, set
\code{options(vernissage.unicode = FALSE)}.
}

